{
  "messages": [
    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Which ONE of the following metals will reduce $\\text{Cd}^{2+}(\\text{aq})$ to $\\text{Cd}(\\text{s})$, but will NOT reduce $\\text{Mn}^{2+}(\\text{aq})$ to $\\text{Mn}(\\text{s})$?\n\nA $\\text{Zn}$\nB $\\text{Ag}$\nC $\\text{Ni}$\nD $\\text{Mg}$"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "reasoning": "This is a standard reduction potential comparison. Use E°cell = E°red(target ion/metal) − E°red(candidate metal ion/metal); a positive E°cell means the target ions can be reduced spontaneously. Fade in the first comparison using zinc and cadmium, then ask the learner to interpret the sign and compare with manganese.",
      "content": "Let’s work through the conditions one at a time. A metal will reduce another metal’s ions spontaneously if the resulting cell potential is positive:\n$$E^\\circ_{\\text{cell}}=E^\\circ_{\\text{red}}(\\text{ions being reduced})-E^\\circ_{\\text{red}}(\\text{candidate metal couple})$$\n\nFor Zn reducing $\\text{Cd}^{2+}$, the table gives $E^\\circ_{\\text{red}}(\\text{Cd}^{2+}/\\text{Cd})=-0.40\\text{ V}$ and $E^\\circ_{\\text{red}}(\\text{Zn}^{2+}/\\text{Zn})=-0.76\\text{ V}$. What value do you get for $E^\\circ_{\\text{cell}}$ in this first comparison, and what does its sign tell you?"
    },
    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I get +0.36 V, so Zn can reduce Cd²⁺. But for Mn²⁺, Zn’s −0.76 V is more negative than Mn’s −1.18 V, so maybe it reduces Mn²⁺ too?"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "reasoning": "The learner correctly calculates the cadmium cell potential, but reverses the ordering of −0.76 and −1.18: −0.76 V is more positive. Calculate the manganese cell potential to settle the second condition, then consolidate the choice and exam method. [TOOL CHECK: calculator(expression=\"-0.40 - (-0.76)\") -> 0.36] [TOOL CHECK: calculator(expression=\"-1.18 - (-0.76)\") -> -0.42]",
      "content": "Your $+0.36\\text{ V}$ result is right, so Zn can reduce $\\text{Cd}^{2+}$. The comparison with manganese needs one sign-order check: $-0.76\\text{ V}$ is **more positive** than $-1.18\\text{ V}$, not more negative.\n\nFor Zn and $\\text{Mn}^{2+}$:\n$$E^\\circ_{\\text{cell}}=-1.18-(-0.76)=-0.42\\text{ V}$$\nBecause this is negative, the reaction is not spontaneous: Zn will **not** reduce $\\text{Mn}^{2+}$. Therefore the correct choice is **A, Zn**.\n\n### 📖 Formula Breakdown (How It Works):\n* **The Formula:** $E^\\circ_{\\text{cell}}=E^\\circ_{\\text{red}}(\\text{ions being reduced})-E^\\circ_{\\text{red}}(\\text{candidate metal couple})$\n* **What the letters mean:**\n  * $E^\\circ_{\\text{cell}}$: the standard cell potential for the proposed reaction.\n  * $E^\\circ_{\\text{red}}$: the standard reduction potential from the table for a reduction half-reaction.\n* **Why we use it:** A positive cell potential means the proposed reaction is spontaneous. The chosen metal must give a positive value with $\\text{Cd}^{2+}$, but a zero or negative value with $\\text{Mn}^{2+}$.\n\n🎯 **Exam Tip:** Show both comparisons and their signs: Zn/Cd gives $+0.36\\text{ V}$, while Zn/Mn gives $-0.42\\text{ V}$. Be careful when ordering negative numbers: $-0.76$ is greater (more positive) than $-1.18$."
    }
  ],
  "meta": {
